  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a light-driven catalytic cycle and the corresponding free-energy profile. On the left, a catalyst labeled C is excited by light in the presence of a reagent marked R¹⁺ to form an intermediate I¹. A second photoinduced step involving R²⁺ converts this to another intermediate, I². From there, the cycle closes by regenerating the catalyst C while producing an additional C-containing product state, suggesting catalyst turnover through two sequential photochemical events.\n\nThe right side translates this scheme into an energy landscape. The vertical axis is Gibbs free energy, and the red curve shows a multistep reaction pathway with several minima and barriers. The starting state appears to be a complex of R¹, R², and C. Two lightning symbols and upward arrows mark photoexcitation steps that lift the system to higher-energy states, enabling it to cross otherwise significant barriers. The labeled wells correspond to the key intermediates, including a combined I¹ + R² state and I², before reaching a final lower-energy state labeled C + C. Overall, the figure emphasizes how successive light inputs drive the system through high-energy intermediates to complete a catalytic cycle that would be less accessible thermally.",
